WEBVTT

NOTE SA1 Topic 4.2: Users, Groups and OUs. Timings are estimates at 150 words a minute; re-sync after recording.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Welcome to Topic 4.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.850
2, Users, Groups and OUs.

3
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.300
In Topic 4.

4
00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:07.950
1 you built the domain sa1.

5
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.400
lab.

6
00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:11.250
Now you fill it.

7
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.900
You will learn the difference between the
built-in containers and organizational

8
00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:20.150
units, and how to design OUs around what
administrators need.

9
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.200
You will read distinguished names and
protect OUs from being deleted by

10
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:26.650
accident.

11
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:32.500
You will create users with the right logon
names, in Active Directory Users and

12
00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:35.950
Computers, in the Administrative Center
and in PowerShell.

13
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:42.600
Then you will manage accounts day to day,
and finish with group types, scopes and a

14
00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:44.450
nesting pattern called AGDLP.

15
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.100
Open a brand new domain and you already
see several folders.

16
00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.350
Most of them are containers.

17
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.600
Their names start with CN.

18
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:58.650
Users holds new users and groups by
default, including Domain Admins and

19
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:00.100
Domain Users.

20
00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.350
Computers holds newly joined computers.

21
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:08.600
Containers come with the domain, and you
cannot link Group Policy to them or create

22
00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:10.050
your own.

23
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.700
Organizational units, OUs, start with OU.

24
00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:18.950
You create them, you can link Group Policy
to them, delegate control of them, and

25
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.400
nest them.

26
00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:25.050
Domain Controllers is the one OU that
exists from the start.

27
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:30.500
Build OUs for the jobs administrators do,
not to copy the org chart.

28
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.950
Our tree starts with an OU called
Lakeshore.

29
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.600
Staff holds Ann, Ben and Carla.

30
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:40.050
Students holds the students, and in Topic
4.

31
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:44.700
3 the help desk gets the right to reset
passwords there.

32
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.750
Groups keeps every group in one place.

33
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.200
Servers and Workstations will get
different policies later.

34
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.650
Every object has a distinguished name, or
DN.

35
00:01:54.900 --> 00:02:00.900
For Dev Patel it is CN equals Dev Patel,
OU equals Students, OU equals Lakeshore,

36
00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:03.550
DC equals sa1, DC equals lab.

37
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:08.200
The object comes first, then each parent
up to the domain.

38
00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:11.250
Move the object and the DN changes.

39
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.700
To create an OU in Active Directory Users
and Computers, right-click the domain,

40
00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.150
sa1.

41
00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.800
lab, and choose New, Organizational Unit.

42
00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:24.250
Create in shows where the OU will go.

43
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.100
Type the name, Lakeshore.

44
00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:29.950
Notice the check box Protect container
from accidental deletion.

45
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:34.600
It is ticked by default, and you should
leave it ticked.

46
00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:38.450
For a child OU like Staff, right-click
Lakeshore instead.

47
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:44.300
If you ever really need to delete a
protected OU, turn on View, Advanced

48
00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:49.750
Features, open its properties, and clear
the box on the Object tab first.

49
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.800
Here is the same job in PowerShell.

50
00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:57.050
New-ADOrganizationalUnit with dash Name
Lakeshore and dash Path, the domain,

51
00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.900
creates the top OU.

52
00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:02.750
Store its distinguished name in a variable
called root.

53
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.400
Then one foreach loop creates the five
child OUs under it.

54
00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:11.250
Get-ADOrganizationalUnit with dash
SearchBase lists only what is under

55
00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.500
Lakeshore, with each distinguished name.

56
00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:16.550
Finally, try to remove the Servers OU.

57
00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:18.800
You get Access is denied.

58
00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:24.650
The cmdlet protects new OUs by default,
just like the check box in ADUC.

59
00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.500
That is a safety net you want to keep.

60
00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:31.150
Right-click Staff and choose New, User.

61
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:36.200
Type the first and last name, and the Full
name fills in.

62
00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:42.450
Full name is the object name, so Ann's DN
starts with CN equals Ann Lee.

63
00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:49.100
User logon name plus the suffix is her
user principal name, or UPN: alee at sa1.

64
00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:50.550
lab.

65
00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.600
It must be unique in the forest.

66
00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:58.650
The pre-Windows 2000 logon name is the
sAMAccountName, SA1 backslash alee, with

67
00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:00.500
at most twenty characters.

68
00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:05.550
On the next page, type the password shown
in your NetLab pod.

69
00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:10.200
In our labs, clear User must change
password at next logon.

70
00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:13.250
For real new users, keep it ticked.

71
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.700
Active Directory Administrative Center, or
ADAC, is the newer console for the same

72
00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:20.150
directory.

73
00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:27.200
You navigate with a breadcrumb at the top
and act from the Tasks pane on the right.

74
00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:32.650
Its best feature for learners sits at the
bottom: the Windows PowerShell History

75
00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.100
Viewer.

76
00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:40.750
Open it, and every action you take appears
as the real cmdlet with the values you

77
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.200
typed.

78
00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:48.050
Create Ben Ortiz, and you see New-ADUser
with his name, path and logon name.

79
00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.700
Copy a line and reuse it.

80
00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:55.750
Commands that only read data are hidden
until you tick Show All.

81
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:02.000
To create users in PowerShell, first read
the password shown in your NetLab pod with

82
00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:03.450
Read-Host dash AsSecureString.

83
00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:08.500
You see only stars, and the password never
appears in your script.

84
00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:15.150
Then put the user's details in a hash
table and pass it to New-ADUser with the

85
00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:16.600
at sign.

86
00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:18.450
That is called splatting.

87
00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:21.500
Two keys matter most: AccountPassword and
Enabled.

88
00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:25.750
Leave them out and the account is created,
but disabled.

89
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.800
Get-ADUser with dash Filter star and dash
SearchBase lists everyone under Lakeshore,

90
00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:33.050
with UPN and Enabled status.

91
00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:37.300
Most daily work starts with a right-click
on a user.

92
00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:42.350
Disable Account blocks sign-in but keeps
the account, its groups and its

93
00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:43.800
permissions.

94
00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:50.050
Use it when someone leaves or goes on
leave, and Enable Account brings it back.

95
00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:56.300
Reset Password sets a new password, and
the dialog can also unlock the account in

96
00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:57.750
the same step.

97
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.200
Move sends the account to another OU, and
drag and drop works too.

98
00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:09.050
And in Properties, the Account tab shows
the logon names, the Unlock account check

99
00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.300
box and Account is disabled.

100
00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:14.350
Each of those tasks has a cmdlet.

101
00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.200
Disable-ADAccount turns off dpatel.

102
00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:20.850
Get-ADUser with the filter Enabled equals
false finds every disabled account.

103
00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:25.500
You will see Guest, krbtgt and
DefaultAccount too, because they are

104
00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:26.950
disabled by default.

105
00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:31.600
Set-ADAccountPassword with dash Reset is
an administrator reset, using the password

106
00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:33.450
you stored with Read-Host.

107
00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.700
Enable-ADAccount turns dpatel back on.

108
00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:39.150
Unlock-ADAccount clears a lockout but
keeps the password.

109
00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:44.600
Move-ADObject takes the object's DN and a
target path, and afterwards the DN

110
00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:46.050
changes.

111
00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:50.300
Every one of these works on any user you
name.

112
00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:53.750
Every group has a type and a scope.

113
00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.400
The type is security or distribution.

114
00:06:56.650 --> 00:07:01.050
Security groups can be used in
permissions, and distribution groups are

115
00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:02.500
only email lists.

116
00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:05.950
The scope decides what a group can
contain.

117
00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:11.800
A global group holds users, computers and
other global groups from the same domain,

118
00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:16.850
so use it to collect people with the same
role, like GG-Faculty.

119
00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:22.300
A domain local group can hold users,
global groups and universal groups from

120
00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:28.550
anywhere in the forest, and you use it for
permissions in its own domain, like

121
00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.000
DL-Shared-Read.

122
00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:33.450
Universal groups matter in forests with
many domains.

123
00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:36.900
Domain Admins and Domain Users are global
groups.

124
00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:39.950
AGDLP is the order to nest groups.

125
00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:43.000
A for accounts: Carla, Dev and Erin.

126
00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:48.050
G for global groups: Carla goes into
GG-Faculty, the students into GG-Students.

127
00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:52.700
DL for domain local: both global groups
become members of DL-Shared-Read.

128
00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:56.950
P for permission: only DL-Shared-Read gets
Read on the share.

129
00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:58.400
Why bother?

130
00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:04.650
People change all the time, so you add and
remove them in one global group.

131
00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.700
Each resource keeps one simple permission
entry.

132
00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:12.750
In a forest with many domains, universal
groups go in the middle.

133
00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:18.600
To create a group in ADUC, right-click the
Groups OU and choose New, Group.

134
00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:21.650
Type the group name, for example GG-IT.

135
00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:24.300
The pre-Windows 2000 name fills in.

136
00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:28.950
Our prefixes, GG and DL, show the scope at
a glance.

137
00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:34.400
Under Group scope, choose Global for the
GG groups and Domain local for

138
00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:35.850
DL-Shared-Read.

139
00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:38.900
Under Group type, keep Security, the
default.

140
00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:44.350
To add members, open the group's
properties and use the Members tab, or

141
00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:48.200
right-click a user and choose Add to a
group.

142
00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:53.250
In PowerShell, New-ADGroup needs a name
and a dash GroupScope: Global, DomainLocal

143
00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:54.700
or Universal.

144
00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:59.350
Add dash GroupCategory Security and a path
to the Groups OU.

145
00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:04.800
One loop makes the four global groups, and
one more command makes DL-Shared-Read.

146
00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:09.050
Add-ADGroupMember puts dpatel and emoss
into GG-Students, then GG-Faculty and

147
00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:10.500
GG-Students into DL-Shared-Read.

148
00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:13.150
Get-ADGroupMember confirms the two group
members.

149
00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:17.000
Now try the wrong direction: put
DL-Shared-Read into GG-IT.

150
00:09:17.250 --> 00:09:23.250
You get a red error, because a global
group cannot contain a domain local group.

151
00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:24.700
Four common mistakes.

152
00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:28.150
One: leaving new accounts in the Users
container.

153
00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:34.800
Create them in an OU, and move any strays,
because Group Policy cannot be linked to

154
00:09:35.050 --> 00:09:36.250
Users.

155
00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:40.100
Two: a delete fails with protected from
accidental deletion.

156
00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:42.750
That is the safety catch working.

157
00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:47.800
Clear it on the Object tab only when you
really mean it.

158
00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:52.050
Three: running New-ADUser with no password
and no dash Enabled.

159
00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:54.700
The account exists but is disabled.

160
00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:58.950
Four: giving permissions to users, or
nesting the wrong way.

161
00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:00.400
Follow AGDLP.

162
00:10:00.650 --> 00:10:05.850
Time to check yourself, using the
Lakeshore tree you build in the lab.

163
00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:11.700
First, why does Lakeshore have a Students
OU, instead of keeping students in the

164
00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:13.150
Users container?

165
00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:16.600
Second, Erin Moss is in the Students OU.

166
00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:18.850
What is her distinguished name?

167
00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:22.300
Third, can GG-Students be a member of
DL-Shared-Read?

168
00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:25.750
And can it work the other way round?

169
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:30.400
Fourth, you ran New-ADUser with only a
name and a sAMAccountName.

170
00:10:30.650 --> 00:10:34.250
Where is the account, and can it sign in?

171
00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:37.300
Pause the video and answer all four.

172
00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:39.150
Here are the answers.

173
00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:45.400
One: an OU can receive Group Policy and
delegated control, so the help desk can

174
00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:47.250
reset student passwords there.

175
00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:49.100
The Users container cannot.

176
00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:54.550
Two: CN equals Erin Moss, OU equals
Students, OU equals Lakeshore, DC equals

177
00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:56.400
sa1, DC equals lab.

178
00:10:56.650 --> 00:11:00.650
Three: yes, a domain local group can
contain global groups.

179
00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:06.900
No, a global group can contain only users,
computers and other global groups from the

180
00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:08.350
same domain.

181
00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:14.200
Four: the account is in the Users
container, and it is disabled, because it

182
00:11:14.450 --> 00:11:18.050
has no password and you did not enable it.

183
00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:20.700
Let me show you on DC01.

184
00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:26.150
In ADUC I create Lakeshore and Staff, and
the protection box stays ticked.

185
00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:30.000
PowerShell adds the other four OUs in one
loop.

186
00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:33.450
When I try to delete Servers, it refuses.

187
00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:40.100
I add Ann Lee in ADUC, then Ben Ortiz in
the Administrative Center, and the history

188
00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:43.150
viewer shows the New-ADUser command it
ran.

189
00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:45.400
Carla goes in with PowerShell.

190
00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:48.450
I build the groups and nest them.

191
00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:54.300
Finally I disable Dev Patel, find him with
a filter, and enable him again.

192
00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:55.750
To wrap up.

193
00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:01.600
Containers hold the defaults, and OUs take
Group Policy and delegation, so design OUs

194
00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:04.250
around administration and keep them
protected.

195
00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:09.300
Each user has a full name, a UPN like alee
at sa1.

196
00:12:09.550 --> 00:12:12.750
lab, and a sAMAccountName like SA1
backslash alee.

197
00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:18.600
Every GUI task has a cmdlet, and the
Administrative Center shows you which one.

198
00:12:18.850 --> 00:12:20.450
Nest groups with AGDLP.

199
00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:21.900
In Lab 4.

200
00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:26.950
2 you build this exact tree on DC01, in
about sixty minutes.

201
00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:31.200
Later labs depend on it, so type the names
carefully.
